 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S444-022\", \"clauses\": {\"order\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F06\", \"template\": \"Order these fractions from smallest to largest: {display_str}.\", \"reasoning\": \"F06 prints the task Order over a list of unit fractions drawn from F11 and F13.\"}, \"unit_fractions\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F11\", \"template\": \"{num}/{den}\", \"reasoning\": \"num is fixed to the member 1 and den ranges over 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 and 8, so every F11 value is a unit fraction.\"}, \"smallest_to_largest\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F06\", \"template\": \"Order these fractions from smallest to largest: {display_str}.\", \"reasoning\": \"F06 names the smallest-to-largest direction. Its key-direction gap is recorded under failing_families and does not remove the producibility of the clause.\"}, \"vice_versa\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F05\", \"template\": \"Order these fractions from largest to smallest: {display_str}.\", \"reasoning\": \"F05 gives the reverse direction over unit fractions from F11.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F02\", \"F03\", \"F05\", \"F06\", \"F07\", \"F09\", \"F10\", \"F12\", \"F13\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"Arrange these fractions from greatest to least: {display_str}.\", \"Arrange these fractions from least to greatest: {display_str}.\", \"Order these fractions from largest to smallest: {display_str}.\", \"Order these fractions from smallest to largest: {display_str}.\", \"Place all the {frac_lbl}s from {direction_words}: {answer}.\", \"Which list shows these fractions ordered from largest to smallest: {display_str}?\", \"Which list shows these fractions ordered from smallest to largest: {display_str}?\", \"{answer}\", \"{member}\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"Rule 2 and the failing set rest on the domains as declared. (1) F13 is the members list for distractors and answer choices, and it mixes two kinds of value: 4-fraction unit lists and single improper fractions (the N/1 and N/M members with N greater than M). Improper fraction is a NOT_YET_KNOWN term and falls outside unit fractions, and a single improper fraction is offered as an answer choice in an ordering item. F13 fails. (2) F03, F06 and F10 nest F13 inside display_str, so their question text can print one improper fraction as the list to be ordered. Those three fail on that ground. (3) F12 is the keyed answer. Its result values contain every four-denominator set in both orders, and each value is monotone in one direction. value_derivations derive answer only from numerator and denominator, and no direction value is declared for it. The direction of the question template (F02, F03, F05, F06, F09, F10) is therefore not tied to the direction of the key, so a question can ask for one order while its key is in the other. Those six question families and F12 fail on that ground. (4) F07 pairs direction_words, derived from direction, with answer, which is not derived from direction. Its hint can name one order and print the key in the other order. F07 fails. (5) F02, F05 and F09 nest only F11, which yields single fractions. Whether display_str joins several values into a list is not shown in the packet, so this is noted rather than counted as a separate failure. (6) F01 prints fraction, which is in cumulative_vocab. F04 and F08 are correct hints within the lc's vocabulary. F11 unit fractions are within the lc's scope. Denominator 8 for grade 2 quarter 4 cannot be checked from the packet and is not counted.\"}",
